\subsection{An exact first-level comparison on the triangle}
The report's empirical question compares lift-and-project relaxations on mixed-integer instances. Its benchmark figures are historical source observations, not measurements performed here. We test the stronger-relaxation route on one fully specified $0$--$1$ instance: maximize $x_1+x_2+x_3$ over the triangle stable-set relaxation
\[
P=\{0\le x_i\le1:x_i+x_j\le1\quad(i\ne j)\}.
\]
The original relaxation has optimum $3/2$ at $(1/2,1/2,1/2)$, because summing the three edge inequalities gives that upper bound. The integer optimum is 1.
\begin{proposition}
Multiplying all defining inequalities by each $x_i$ and $1-x_i$, and consistently linearizing products with $x_i^2=x_i$, projects exactly to the integer hull $\{x\ge0:\sum_i x_i\le1\}$. The single-coordinate disjunction $x_1=0$ or $x_1=1$ also yields that hull for this instance.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Write $y_{ij}=y_{ji}$ for $x_ix_j$. The products of nonnegativity constraints imply $y_{ij}\ge0$. Multiplying $1-x_i-x_j\ge0$ by $x_i$ and linearizing gives $-y_{ij}\ge0$, hence all three distinct-pair moments vanish. Multiplying $1-x_1-x_2\ge0$ by $1-x_3$ now gives
\[
1-x_1-x_2-x_3+y_{13}+y_{23}\ge0,
\]
so $\sum_i x_i\le1$. Conversely every point of this simplex is a convex combination of $0,e_1,e_2,e_3$; their actual products satisfy all multiplied inequalities. Since the lifted constraints are linear, the same convex combination is a feasible lift. Thus projection equals the simplex.
For the disjunction, the branch $x_1=0$ is the two-dimensional simplex $x_2,x_3\ge0,x_2+x_3\le1$, with vertices $0,e_2,e_3$. The branch $x_1=1$ forces $x_2=x_3=0$. The convex hull of the two branches is therefore the same simplex.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Verification and remaining gaps}
The feasible binary vectors and objective values were enumerated; the moment cancellations and branch hulls were checked with exact coefficients. This instance closes the entire gap under both procedures and therefore supplies no evidence of a strict practical advantage of one over the other. The attempted inference that more lifted variables necessarily imply a strictly better projected bound fails here. Runtime, numerical behavior, valid inequalities on general instances and the requested empirical comparison remain untested. This known small example is not a new benchmark result.

